Let $\{p(\cdot\mid\theta) : \theta\in\Theta\}$ be a regular exponential family on a sample space $\mathcal{X}$ with sufficient statistic $T:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^d$, log-partition function $A(\theta)$, and densities $p(x\mid\theta)=h(x)\exp(\langle\theta,T(x)\rangle-A(\theta))$ with respect to some base measure. Let $A^*$ denote the convex conjugate of $A$, so that the mean parameter $\mu=\nabla A(\theta)$ and the natural parameter $\theta=\nabla A^*(\mu)$ are in bijection, and for any two parameters one has the duality $\mathrm{KL}(p(\cdot\mid\theta_1)\,\|\,p(\cdot\mid\theta_2)) = B_{A^*}(\mu_1;\mu_2)$, where $B_{A^*}(u;v)=A^*(u)-A^*(v)-\langle\nabla A^*(v),u-v\rangle$ is the Bregman divergence induced by $A^*$.

Fix a true parameter $\theta^*\in\Theta$ with mean $\mu^*=\mathbb{E}_{\theta^*}[T(X)]$, and let $D_n=(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ be i.i.d.\ samples from $p(\cdot\mid\theta^*)$. Define:
\begin{itemize}
\item the maximum likelihood estimator in mean coordinates: $\hat\mu^{\mathrm{MLE}}_n=\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n T(X_i)$;
\item the conjugate MAP estimator with hyperparameters $(n_0,\mu_0)$ (with $n_0>0$ and $\mu_0$ in the interior of the mean-parameter domain): $\hat\mu^{\mathrm{MAP}}_n=\dfrac{n_0\mu_0+\sum_{i=1}^n T(X_i)}{n_0+n}$.
\end{itemize}

Provide non-asymptotic, deterministic (i.e.\ no remaining expectation over $D_n$) upper bounds on
$$\mathbb{E}_{D_n}\bigl[B_{A^*}(\mu^*;\hat\mu^{\mathrm{MLE}}_n)\bigr] \quad\text{and}\quad \mathbb{E}_{D_n}\bigl[B_{A^*}(\mu^*;\hat\mu^{\mathrm{MAP}}_n)\bigr],$$
that are valid for finite $n$, hold across a broad class of regular exponential families (including the regime where $n$ is small relative to the dimension $d$), and recover the correct order of the asymptotic Fisher-information rate as $n\to\infty$.

